1
00:00:00,003 --> 00:00:09,592
Today we're going to talk
about the four fundamental

2
00:00:09,592 --> 00:00:15,390
building blocks from which all

3
00:00:15,390 --> 00:00:21,860
Android applications are built.

4
00:00:23,590 --> 00:00:27,390
These building blocks
are implemented as Java classes.

5
00:00:27,390 --> 00:00:30,700
And the first of these building blocks,
is the activity class.

6
00:00:30,700 --> 00:00:35,240
Now, that's the main class that users
see when they run an application.

7
00:00:35,240 --> 00:00:38,840
Activities are designed to provide
a graphical user interface, or

8
00:00:38,840 --> 00:00:41,180
a gui, to the user.

9
00:00:41,180 --> 00:00:46,250
And this enables users to give and receive
information to and from an application.

10
00:00:46,250 --> 00:00:50,810
The remaining three components
work behind the scenes, so

11
00:00:50,810 --> 00:00:52,320
they don't have user interfaces.

12
00:00:53,370 --> 00:00:58,010
And these components include services for
supporting long-running or

13
00:00:58,010 --> 00:01:00,390
in the background operations.

14
00:01:00,390 --> 00:01:06,540
Broadcast receivers that listen for and
respond to events that happen on a device.

15
00:01:06,540 --> 00:01:11,530
And content providers which allow multiple
applications to store and share data.

16
00:01:13,010 --> 00:01:17,190
Applications are typically created from
multiple collaborating components.

17
00:01:17,190 --> 00:01:20,340
Which Android starts up and
runs as necessary.

18
00:01:21,480 --> 00:01:24,240
And each of these components
serves a different purpose in

19
00:01:24,240 --> 00:01:25,940
the Android ecosystem.

20
00:01:25,940 --> 00:01:30,020
And therefore has its own entry point and
its own APIs.

21
00:01:30,020 --> 00:01:33,180
Let's take a look at each of
these components one at a time.

22
00:01:35,590 --> 00:01:38,470
First, let's talk about
the activity class.

23
00:01:38,470 --> 00:01:39,350
As I said earlier,

24
00:01:39,350 --> 00:01:43,730
this class is designed to present
a graphical user interface to the user.

25
00:01:43,730 --> 00:01:47,640
And to capture the user's
interaction through that interface.

26
00:01:47,640 --> 00:01:49,930
As a general rule of thumb,

27
00:01:49,930 --> 00:01:54,430
an activity should support a single
focused thing that the user can do.

28
00:01:54,430 --> 00:01:57,220
A single thing like
dialing a phone number, or

29
00:01:57,220 --> 00:02:01,270
entering contact information for
a single person and so on.

30
00:02:01,270 --> 00:02:04,820
Now, of course as device screens,
especially those on tablets,

31
00:02:04,820 --> 00:02:09,140
get larger and larger, what we mean
by a single focused thing that

32
00:02:09,140 --> 00:02:11,620
the user can do is of
course bound to change.

33
00:02:12,900 --> 00:02:15,000
Now as an example of an activity,

34
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:17,550
let's take a look at
the phone dialer application.

35
00:02:19,140 --> 00:02:22,989
Here, I'll show you the phone
application running on an actual device.

36
00:02:24,900 --> 00:02:28,420
You remember from earlier lessons
that the phone application can open

37
00:02:28,420 --> 00:02:31,840
a user interface with multiple tabs.

38
00:02:31,840 --> 00:02:35,818
One for a phone dialer, one for
the call log, and one for a contact list.

39
00:02:35,818 --> 00:02:38,040
On Android 4.2,

40
00:02:38,040 --> 00:02:43,230
this application source code is actually
part of the context application.

41
00:02:43,230 --> 00:02:49,060
And it's written in a file
called Dial tacksActivity.java.

42
00:02:49,060 --> 00:02:50,440
Here, I'll open that file.

43
00:02:52,760 --> 00:02:58,036
As you can see, the Dial tacksActivity
class is a subclass of activity.

44
00:03:03,929 --> 00:03:07,598
And the comments here,
explain that this activity is supposed to

45
00:03:07,598 --> 00:03:12,050
display a tab containing the phone,
the phone dialer, the call log.

46
00:03:12,050 --> 00:03:14,410
And the contacts list
that I mentioned earlier.

47
00:03:15,690 --> 00:03:19,952
Let me pause here, and give you a chance
to explore this source code for yourself.

48
00:03:23,725 --> 00:03:27,140
The next component is the service class.

49
00:03:27,140 --> 00:03:30,210
Unlike activities,
services run in the background.

50
00:03:30,210 --> 00:03:33,850
So there's no need for
services to have user interfaces.

51
00:03:33,850 --> 00:03:37,790
Instead services have two main purposes,
one they can

52
00:03:37,790 --> 00:03:42,750
perform long running operations
typically away from the main UI thread.

53
00:03:42,750 --> 00:03:44,870
And two, they provide a way for

54
00:03:44,870 --> 00:03:49,750
different processes to request
operations and share data.

55
00:03:49,750 --> 00:03:54,170
Now for an example of a service, let's
take a look at the music application.

56
00:03:55,790 --> 00:03:59,270
So here I'll show you the music
application running on a device.

57
00:04:01,060 --> 00:04:04,100
As you can see, the music application
has a number of different user

58
00:04:04,100 --> 00:04:05,080
interface screens.

59
00:04:06,220 --> 00:04:08,090
That show for example your music.

60
00:04:09,090 --> 00:04:12,340
Showing the songs performed
by a single artist.

61
00:04:12,340 --> 00:04:14,490
Letting you select one
song by that artist.

62
00:04:15,570 --> 00:04:17,760
And finally letting you
play that one song.

63
00:04:19,160 --> 00:04:20,360
Now if you start playing the song,

64
00:04:20,360 --> 00:04:25,580
however, and then decide to back up and
see what other songs that artist has.

65
00:04:25,580 --> 00:04:29,390
Or if you want to do something totally
different, like check your email.

66
00:04:29,390 --> 00:04:31,790
You probably don't want
the music to stop playing.

67
00:04:32,870 --> 00:04:37,960
So Android handles this by using
a service to play the music.

68
00:04:37,960 --> 00:04:40,310
Now here I'll go to
the music application and

69
00:04:40,310 --> 00:04:43,957
open the MediaPlaybackservice.java file.

70
00:04:50,750 --> 00:04:56,400
As you can see, the MediaPlayback
service is a subclass of service.

71
00:04:56,400 --> 00:05:01,380
And the comments explain that this
service allows the music to keep playing,

72
00:05:01,380 --> 00:05:04,110
even if the user switches
between different activities.

73
00:05:05,260 --> 00:05:07,070
Again, let me pause here and

74
00:05:07,070 --> 00:05:10,060
give you a chance to explore
this source code for yourself.

75
00:05:13,650 --> 00:05:16,700
The next component is BroadcastReceiver.

76
00:05:16,700 --> 00:05:20,410
BroadcastReceivers listen for
and respond to events.

77
00:05:20,410 --> 00:05:24,110
And essentially they play the role
of the subscriber in the publish

78
00:05:24,110 --> 00:05:25,260
subscribe pattern.

79
00:05:26,740 --> 00:05:30,230
In Android, events are represented
by the intent class which we'll

80
00:05:30,230 --> 00:05:32,360
talk about more in later classes.

81
00:05:33,990 --> 00:05:35,850
Publishers create these intents and

82
00:05:35,850 --> 00:05:41,540
then broadcast them using a method like
the context classes send broadcast.

83
00:05:41,540 --> 00:05:46,450
Now once broadcasts these intents are then
routed to the broadcast receivers that

84
00:05:46,450 --> 00:05:51,300
have subscribed to or registered for
those specific intents.

85
00:05:51,300 --> 00:05:54,620
At which point they can
respond to the event.

86
00:05:54,620 --> 00:05:59,500
Now the messaging application is one
example application that makes use of

87
00:05:59,500 --> 00:06:01,040
broadcast receivers.

88
00:06:01,040 --> 00:06:02,660
Let's take a look at that application.

89
00:06:04,880 --> 00:06:07,610
So let's imagine that somewhere
out there in the world,

90
00:06:07,610 --> 00:06:11,180
someone decides that they want
to sent me an SMS message.

91
00:06:12,210 --> 00:06:15,240
That SMS message will be created, and

92
00:06:15,240 --> 00:06:18,500
sent, and
will flow through the telephone network.

93
00:06:18,500 --> 00:06:21,020
And eventually arrive at my phone.

94
00:06:21,020 --> 00:06:27,010
And when it does, Android will put a
notification icon in the notification bar

95
00:06:27,010 --> 00:06:31,210
to let me know that an SMS
message has arrived for me.

96
00:06:31,210 --> 00:06:34,340
Now, of course, you can't know exactly
when you're going to receive such

97
00:06:34,340 --> 00:06:35,470
a message.

98
00:06:35,470 --> 00:06:42,490
So Android has some software that just
sits and waits for SMS messages to arrive.

99
00:06:42,490 --> 00:06:50,930
And when they do, that software
broadcasts an, SMS_received, intent.

100
00:06:50,930 --> 00:06:55,510
And there is another broadcast receiver
that is listening for that intent.

101
00:06:56,790 --> 00:07:01,630
And that broadcast receiver will
eventually receive the intent and will

102
00:07:01,630 --> 00:07:09,330
then start up a service that will download
and store the incoming SMS message.

103
00:07:09,330 --> 00:07:11,650
Now here I'll go to
the MMS application and

104
00:07:11,650 --> 00:07:15,989
open one of its broadcast receivers,
SMSReceiver.Java.

105
00:07:15,989 --> 00:07:22,550
As you can see, the SMSReceiver class
is a sub class of broadcast receiver.

106
00:07:22,550 --> 00:07:26,590
And the comments explain that
this broadcast receiver hands off

107
00:07:26,590 --> 00:07:31,330
the incoming SMS message to a service
which take cares of the downloading and

108
00:07:31,330 --> 00:07:33,070
storage of the message.

109
00:07:33,070 --> 00:07:38,370
Now once more, let's pause here and
explore the source,

110
00:07:38,370 --> 00:07:39,479
the source code for yourself.

111
00:07:42,090 --> 00:07:45,060
The last component is content provider.

112
00:07:45,060 --> 00:07:49,040
Content provider allows applications
to store and share data.

113
00:07:49,040 --> 00:07:52,010
Content providers use
a database style interface.

114
00:07:52,010 --> 00:07:54,380
But they're more than just databases.

115
00:07:54,380 --> 00:07:58,230
For example, content providers will
handle the low level details of

116
00:07:58,230 --> 00:08:00,190
interprocess communication.

117
00:08:00,190 --> 00:08:04,390
So that applications running in
separate processes can communicate and

118
00:08:04,390 --> 00:08:07,740
exchange data safely and easily.

119
00:08:07,740 --> 00:08:11,666
The browser application is one
example of an application that uses

120
00:08:11,666 --> 00:08:12,935
content providers.

121
00:08:19,492 --> 00:08:21,490
So here,
I'll start the browser application.

122
00:08:22,670 --> 00:08:25,550
If I click on the icon
next to the address bar,

123
00:08:25,550 --> 00:08:29,580
the browser opens a list of bookmarks or
saved addresses for

124
00:08:29,580 --> 00:08:32,860
websites that I might want to
access quickly in the future.

125
00:08:35,320 --> 00:08:39,680
When a user adds one of these bookmarks
the browser application stores it

126
00:08:39,680 --> 00:08:40,880
in a content provider.

127
00:08:42,090 --> 00:08:45,170
Let's open the browser
application sourcecode and

128
00:08:45,170 --> 00:08:48,530
take a look at
the BrowserProvider.java file.

129
00:08:49,870 --> 00:08:55,230
As you can see, the browser provider
is a sub class of content provider.

130
00:08:55,230 --> 00:08:57,520
And if we nose around
a bit in the sourcecode,

131
00:08:57,520 --> 00:09:01,519
you can see some suggestions that there's
a database table called bookmarks.

132
00:09:02,650 --> 00:09:06,020
Which is used for
storing the bookmarks that the user adds.

133
00:09:06,020 --> 00:09:09,430
I'll pause again here and let you
explore this source code for yourself.

134
00:09:13,270 --> 00:09:17,320
In our last class we looked at
the Hello Android application.

135
00:09:17,320 --> 00:09:21,310
That simple application involved
just a single activity.

136
00:09:21,310 --> 00:09:24,830
Today however, we're going to look
at a more complex application,

137
00:09:24,830 --> 00:09:26,980
in this case comprised of two activities.

138
00:09:28,220 --> 00:09:31,300
And of course once you know how to use
two activities, adding a third and

139
00:09:31,300 --> 00:09:33,960
a fourth and so
on would just be more of the same.

140
00:09:35,100 --> 00:09:37,680
This application is called map location,
and

141
00:09:37,680 --> 00:09:41,970
one of its activities allows
the user to enter a postal address.

142
00:09:41,970 --> 00:09:46,610
That activity also provides a button
that the user can press once he or

143
00:09:46,610 --> 00:09:48,190
she has entered that address.

144
00:09:49,270 --> 00:09:54,180
And pressing that button will then start
up a second activity, Google Maps,

145
00:09:54,180 --> 00:09:59,140
that presents a map centered on
the address that the user just entered.

146
00:09:59,140 --> 00:09:59,705
Let's take a look.

147
00:10:36,773 --> 00:10:42,810
Now this graphic depicts the process of
writing and building Android applications.

148
00:10:42,810 --> 00:10:45,000
First you create the source code and

149
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:49,150
the non-source code resources
that make up your application.

150
00:10:49,150 --> 00:10:53,420
Next, tools compiles your source code and
prepare your resources.

151
00:10:54,530 --> 00:10:57,250
The output of this step
is an Android package, or

152
00:10:57,250 --> 00:11:01,509
APK, which corresponds to your
application's executable.

153
00:11:02,710 --> 00:11:06,950
Next, the APK is digitally signed
to identify you as its developer.

154
00:11:08,060 --> 00:11:12,390
And finally the APK is installed
on a device or emulator and run.

155
00:11:14,680 --> 00:11:18,420
As a developer, your participation
in the build process will

156
00:11:18,420 --> 00:11:21,450
usually involve the following four steps.

157
00:11:21,450 --> 00:11:26,120
Defining resources,
implementing the application classes,

158
00:11:26,120 --> 00:11:30,380
packaging the application and
installing and running the application.

159
00:11:30,380 --> 00:11:32,940
In particular for testing and debugging.

160
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,800
Step one is defining your
application's resources.

161
00:11:37,800 --> 00:11:42,198
As I've said before, Android applications
are more than just source code.

162
00:11:42,198 --> 00:11:45,530
They include non-source
code entities as well.

163
00:11:45,530 --> 00:11:52,420
Things like layout files, strings,
images, menus, animations, and much more.

164
00:11:52,420 --> 00:11:57,410
And managing resources separately from
the application has several benefits,

165
00:11:57,410 --> 00:12:00,920
one of which is that you can
easily alter those resources.

166
00:12:00,920 --> 00:12:04,960
Without having to change or recompile
your application source code in any way.

167
00:12:07,060 --> 00:12:10,070
One common resource type is strings.

168
00:12:10,070 --> 00:12:12,950
In Android,
there are three types of string resources.

169
00:12:12,950 --> 00:12:17,840
Individual strings,
arrays of strings, and plurals.

170
00:12:17,840 --> 00:12:21,890
Now I think strings and a, arrays of
strings are pretty self explanatory.

171
00:12:21,890 --> 00:12:23,710
Let's talk about plurals.

172
00:12:23,710 --> 00:12:26,950
Plurals are basically
string arrays that can be

173
00:12:26,950 --> 00:12:32,690
used to choose specific strings that
are associated with certain quantities.

174
00:12:32,690 --> 00:12:35,980
Such as one book or two books.

175
00:12:37,100 --> 00:12:40,020
And the strings are typically
stored in an XML file in

176
00:12:40,020 --> 00:12:44,360
the res/values directory
of your application.

177
00:12:44,360 --> 00:12:50,740
And the format is, an XML string
tag containing a name attribute and

178
00:12:50,740 --> 00:12:53,510
then containing the actual string itself.

179
00:12:53,510 --> 00:12:56,390
And that actual string can
include formatting and

180
00:12:56,390 --> 00:13:00,060
styling information,
things like HTML tags, for example.

181
00:13:01,380 --> 00:13:04,970
Finally, other resource files
can refer to the strings that

182
00:13:04,970 --> 00:13:11,350
you've defined as ampersand
string/string_name.

183
00:13:11,350 --> 00:13:14,610
In Java code,
you can also access these strings but

184
00:13:14,610 --> 00:13:18,530
this time you do it as
R.string.string_name.

185
00:13:18,530 --> 00:13:25,845
So here we are again in Eclipse.

186
00:13:27,050 --> 00:13:31,350
I've defined some strings for
the map location application.

187
00:13:31,350 --> 00:13:36,680
Let's open these strings.xml file
in the res/values directory.

188
00:13:41,670 --> 00:13:43,455
This file shows two strings.

189
00:13:43,455 --> 00:13:47,410
Show_map_string.

190
00:13:47,410 --> 00:13:49,941
With the value show map and

191
00:13:49,941 --> 00:13:54,900
location_string with
the value Enter Location.

192
00:13:56,080 --> 00:14:01,430
Now I've also defined a second strings.xml
file, this time for the Italian language.

193
00:14:03,260 --> 00:14:07,710
And it's in the res/values-it directory.

194
00:14:07,710 --> 00:14:11,590
And that IT prefix in
the directory name is what

195
00:14:11,590 --> 00:14:16,120
tells Android that this is the string
file for the Italian language.

196
00:14:17,150 --> 00:14:18,690
Now if we look at that file,

197
00:14:18,690 --> 00:14:26,270
we'll see the same two strings show
_map _string, and location_string.

198
00:14:26,270 --> 00:14:31,900
But this time, their values are Italian
words, rather than English words.

199
00:14:34,190 --> 00:14:38,010
In the example I just showed,
if your default language is Italian,

200
00:14:38,010 --> 00:14:41,240
then location string will have the value,
[FOREIGN].

201
00:14:41,240 --> 00:14:47,410
Otherwise, it'll have the value,
enter location.

202
00:14:47,410 --> 00:14:48,294
Let's see that in action.

203
00:14:51,946 --> 00:14:54,900
Now, I'm going to run Map Location twice.

204
00:14:54,900 --> 00:14:59,420
The phone on the left uses English as
its default language while the phone on

205
00:14:59,420 --> 00:15:02,660
the right uses Italian
as its default language.

206
00:15:04,900 --> 00:15:09,409
Now, as you can see, although both phones
are using the same exact source code.

207
00:15:10,500 --> 00:15:13,740
The phone on the left
displays English strings,

208
00:15:13,740 --> 00:15:19,493
while the phone on the right displays

209
00:15:19,493 --> 00:15:27,579
Italian strings.

